WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.540
<v Maya>Welcome to part four, the first part of the second scheduling lecture.

2
00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.290
<v Maya>In lecture one we collected the ingredients.

3
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.430
<v Maya>Now we cook.

4
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.420
<v Maya>The aim is to learn how to analyse or design a workout split.

5
00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:15.760
<v Dev>And the big question left open was how many sets.

6
00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:16.650
<v Maya>Yes.

7
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.570
<v Maya>Picture the board split in two.

8
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.880
<v Maya>The left side holds the master table.

9
00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:31.020
<v Maya>The right side holds the research studies, which supply the factors to consider while choosing a split, just like the factors for prescribing cardio intensity in the cardio lecture.

10
00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:34.840
<v Maya>So we answer it with a master table and a set of research factors.

11
00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.480
<v Maya>The table lists, in order, seven columns.

12
00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.460
<v Maya>Number of main sets per muscle.

13
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:40.910
<v Maya>Recovery period.

14
00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.230
<v Maya>Weekly outlook.

15
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.790
<v Maya>Training frequency.

16
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.020
<v Maya>Split types.

17
00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:46.230
<v Maya>Training level.

18
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.880
<v Maya>And ideal set range.

19
00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.250
<v Maya>The last two are conclusions, so we fill them in at the end.

20
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.680
<v Dev>Is this about weight training only?

21
00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:54.570
<v Maya>Yes.

22
00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.370
<v Maya>A day with no weight training is not a rest day.

23
00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.010
<v Maya>It is free room that can be used for cardio.

24
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.820
<v Maya>Exactly one day a week, Sunday, is the mandatory rest day.

25
00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.060
<v Dev>Let me check I remember the first four columns.

26
00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.220
<v Maya>Go on.

27
00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:13.170
<v Dev>One to three sets, forty eight hours, Monday, Wednesday, Friday, thrice a week.

28
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.050
<v Dev>Four to five sets, seventy two hours, Monday and Thursday, twice a week.

29
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.740
<v Dev>More than five sets, one week, Monday and next Monday, once a week.

30
00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:23.760
<v Maya>Perfect.

31
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:29.960
<v Maya>Remember that sets means main sets per muscle, not warm-up sets and not the total for all exercises.

32
00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:35.380
<v Maya>The same muscle can get its sets from one exercise or several, and it is the muscle's total that counts.

33
00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:36.960
<v Maya>Now the fifth column.

34
00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:42.600
<v Maya>A workout split is how your weight training sessions are arranged across the week, following some logic.

35
00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.110
<v Maya>Different splits follow different logic.

36
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.980
<v Dev>Starting with full body?

37
00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:49.910
<v Maya>Full body workout, shortened to FBW.

38
00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:52.140
<v Maya>Every session trains the whole body.

39
00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:53.560
<v Maya>Next, upper lower.

40
00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:58.120
<v Maya>Upper days train only upper body muscles, back, chest, shoulders and arms.

41
00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.950
<v Maya>Lower days train only lower body muscles.

42
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.780
<v Maya>Upper muscles are not trained on a lower day.

43
00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.410
<v Dev>Push, pull, legs?

44
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.040
<v Maya>That one groups muscles by movement pattern.

45
00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:15.630
<v Maya>A pull day has the pulling movements, rows, lat pulldowns, and biceps curls, which is why back and biceps are trained together.

46
00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:25.250
<v Maya>A push day has the pushing movements, bench press for the chest, vertical push for the front of the shoulder, and triceps, which is why chest, shoulders and triceps go together.

47
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.150
<v Maya>The remaining day is legs.

48
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.800
<v Dev>And the bro split?

49
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.950
<v Maya>Also called the body part split.

50
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.680
<v Maya>One body part per day.

51
00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:38.070
<v Maya>Back on Monday, chest on Tuesday, a gap, then legs, shoulders, arms and a cardio day.

52
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:44.690
<v Maya>Shoulders and arms are kept away from back and chest days, so the same joint is not used on back to back days.

53
00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:47.810
<v Dev>So which split goes with which row of the table?

54
00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.400
<v Maya>Think of it as lining up by frequency.

55
00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:54.520
<v Maya>One to three sets, forty eight hours, thrice a week fits full body.

56
00:02:54.570 --> 00:03:00.320
<v Maya>Monday, Wednesday and Friday full body, with non weight training days between and rest on Sunday.

57
00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:02.850
<v Maya>Each muscle is trained every other day.

58
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.600
<v Dev>Four to five sets?

59
00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:05.680
<v Maya>Upper lower.

60
00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:13.230
<v Maya>Monday upper, Tuesday lower, Wednesday a non weight training day, Thursday upper, Friday lower, Saturday cardio, Sunday rest.

61
00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:20.250
<v Maya>Upper muscles are trained Monday and Thursday, lower muscles Tuesday and Friday, each with seventy two hours in between.

62
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.980
<v Dev>And more than five sets, once a week?

63
00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:25.130
<v Maya>Push pull legs and the bro split.

64
00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:31.610
<v Maya>In the push pull legs example, Monday push, Wednesday pull, Friday legs, Saturday cardio, Sunday rest.

65
00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:34.580
<v Maya>In the bro split, each body part gets one day.

66
00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:36.510
<v Dev>Here's something I got wrong.

67
00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.250
<v Dev>Upper lower has four weight training days.

68
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.320
<v Dev>So the frequency is four?

69
00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:42.610
<v Maya>That is the trap.

70
00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.720
<v Maya>Frequency is per muscle, not per weight training day.

71
00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:47.860
<v Maya>Ask how often the legs are trained.

72
00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:48.800
<v Maya>Twice.

73
00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:52.810
<v Maya>Upper muscles Monday and Thursday, lower muscles Tuesday and Friday.

74
00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.320
<v Maya>This is why frequency was taught first.

75
00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.880
<v Maya>In push pull legs, the latissimus dorsi is trained once, on pull day.

76
00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:06.440
<v Maya>In the bro split, the lats on back day, the pectoralis major on chest day, and the shoulders are each trained once.

77
00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:10.640
<v Maya>Because the frequency is the same, everything we learn applies to both.

78
00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.580
<v Dev>But many people do push pull legs twice, six days a week.

79
00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.030
<v Dev>Is that wrong?

80
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.220
<v Maya>That is the question a student asked.

81
00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:21.170
<v Maya>In that version you do six weight training sessions.

82
00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:25.740
<v Maya>You may be overtraining, and the weekly stimulus may not justify six days.

83
00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:29.700
<v Maya>The board drew push pull legs once a week, but note this is an inference.

84
00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:36.460
<v Maya>By the lecture's own rule, a six day version trains each muscle twice, which would put it in the twice a week category.

85
00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.450
<v Maya>The lecture did not resolve it, so check your course material.

86
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.650
<v Maya>Pause and think.

87
00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:45.570
<v Maya>If a lifter trains upper lower, how many times is the quadriceps trained a week?

88
00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.830
<v Dev>Twice, because lower days are Tuesday and Friday.

89
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.830
<v Dev>Even though there are four weight training days.

90
00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:52.720
<v Maya>Yes.

91
00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.630
<v Maya>Now, the aim of this lecture.

92
00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.810
<v Maya>It is not to criticise any split.

93
00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:02.940
<v Maya>It is to break a myth, the belief that full body is only for beginners, so why would an advanced lifter do it.

94
00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:10.890
<v Maya>The point to prove is that any training level can pick a split from any of the three scenarios, as long as they do the ideal number of sets.

95
00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.840
<v Maya>Not under, not over.

96
00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:16.550
<v Dev>But then why is full body usually given to beginners?

97
00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.660
<v Maya>Because for beginners there is a logical best choice.

98
00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.980
<v Maya>Two pillars of the FITT principle are linked.

99
00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:26.780
<v Maya>Frequency is proportional to one divided by intensity.

100
00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:33.250
<v Maya>In words, when intensity goes up, frequency goes down, and when intensity goes down, frequency can go up.

101
00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.680
<v Dev>Why does that matter for beginners?

102
00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:41.340
<v Maya>Beginners train with low intensity, fifteen to twenty RM, light weight, high reps, learning sets.

103
00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:45.270
<v Maya>Because intensity is low, the muscles do not reach major fatigue.

104
00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:47.990
<v Maya>So high frequency is not a problem for them.

105
00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:51.980
<v Maya>If intensity were high, high frequency would pile on fatigue.

106
00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:54.270
<v Dev>But why should frequency be high?

107
00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.040
<v Dev>Why not just rest?

108
00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:58.360
<v Maya>Because beginners have a lot to learn.

109
00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.180
<v Maya>First, neural pathways.

110
00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:03.020
<v Maya>The gap between two motor neurons is a synapse.

111
00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:07.510
<v Maya>A new movement has a weak pathway, which becomes stronger the more it is repeated.

112
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:11.170
<v Maya>The lecture compared it to crossing a bridge between two mountains.

113
00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:15.610
<v Maya>The first crossing is the hardest, and every later crossing is more confident.

114
00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:17.710
<v Dev>What else is there to learn?

115
00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:23.130
<v Maya>The kinetic chain, for example the arch of the body in the bench press while pressing into the floor.

116
00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:28.210
<v Maya>Proprioception, the proprioceptors working efficiently for each exercise.

117
00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:29.650
<v Maya>Grip and tightness.

118
00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:34.960
<v Maya>And the Valsalva manoeuvre and breathing, when to inhale and exhale in each movement.

119
00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:40.520
<v Dev>So if the chest is trained only once a week, the bench press is practised once a week.

120
00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:41.660
<v Maya>Exactly.

121
00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:46.110
<v Maya>Neural pathways, the kinetic chain and technique take much longer to form.

122
00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:53.390
<v Maya>With full body, three days a week, Monday, Wednesday and Friday, a beginner practises each movement often with light weights.

123
00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:57.640
<v Maya>The usual two to three month beginner phase is then used productively.

124
00:06:57.690 --> 00:07:01.810
<v Dev>So the best choice for beginners is full body, three days a week.

125
00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:02.700
<v Maya>Yes.

126
00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:06.920
<v Maya>And because beginners were taught this way, the industry created a stereotype.

127
00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:13.500
<v Maya>The next parts show intermediate and advanced trainees can also make the most of full body, if they follow the set range.

128
00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:15.820
<v Maya>Now the sixth column, training level.

129
00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:17.510
<v Maya>The lecture ended early.

130
00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:23.260
<v Maya>The remainder will be covered in the next lecture, so the final split selection conclusion is still to come.

131
00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.590
<v Maya>The final level column was not formally completed.

132
00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.780
<v Dev>So what do we know meanwhile?

133
00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:31.450
<v Maya>Beginner, full body, as we just reasoned.

134
00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:41.420
<v Maya>For intermediate and advanced, the lecture's logic points to a split from any of the three scenarios, provided they stay inside the ideal set range for that scenario.

135
00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:43.550
<v Maya>The slides show a fuller picture.

136
00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:45.330
<v Dev>What do the slides show?

137
00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:49.720
<v Maya>Beginner, fifteen to twenty RM, no failure, full body, no split.

138
00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:56.180
<v Maya>Monday, Wednesday and Friday full body, Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday cardio and abs, Sunday rest.

139
00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:02.490
<v Maya>Intermediate, ten to fifteen RM, a double split, upper body and lower body, alternating across two weeks.

140
00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:10.200
<v Maya>Advanced, six to eight RM, a triple split, push, pull and lower body, with four day and six day splits for higher levels.

141
00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:12.880
<v Dev>Wait, six to eight and ten to fifteen.

142
00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.160
<v Dev>That differs from four to six.

143
00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:18.320
<v Maya>Yes, the same difference we flagged in lecture one.

144
00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:23.960
<v Maya>The lecture says four to six RM for stimulating reps, the slides say these other ranges.

145
00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:25.350
<v Maya>Check your course material.

146
00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.020
<v Maya>Five things to remember.

147
00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:33.760
<v Maya>One, the master table links main sets per muscle, recovery, weekly outlook and frequency, then split and level.

148
00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:38.120
<v Dev>Two, one to three sets, forty eight hours, thrice a week, full body.

149
00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:41.590
<v Dev>Four to five, seventy two hours, twice, upper lower.

150
00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:45.420
<v Dev>More than five, one week, once, push pull legs or bro.

151
00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:48.870
<v Maya>Three, frequency is per muscle, not per weight training day.

152
00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:51.950
<v Maya>Upper lower has four days but each muscle twice.

153
00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.070
<v Dev>Four, frequency is proportional to one over intensity.

154
00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:06.370
<v Dev>Beginners use low intensity, so high frequency suits them, to build neural pathways, kinetic chain, proprioception, grip and Valsalva breathing.

155
00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:09.340
<v Maya>Five, full body only for beginners is a myth.

156
00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:12.400
<v Maya>Any level can use any split with the ideal sets.

157
00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:14.310
<v Maya>Next part, the research on why.

